1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,450
You make a compelling point.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:06,175
Why would anyone ever need an embedded hardware simulator?

3
00:00:06,175 --> 00:00:13,900
Why test firmware cleanly in modern C++ when you could instead brick a four-hundred-dollar prototype groovebox at three in the morning?

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:21,850
That distinct, comforting smell of vaporized FR-four fiberglass lets the entire office know you are doing real engineering.

5
00:00:22,512 --> 00:00:27,274
Some people brag about automated CI/CD pipelines.

6
00:00:27,224 --> 00:00:34,762
They love that two thousand wire-accurate binary ComProtocol packets can pass unit tests in eighteen hundredths of a second.

7
00:00:34,762 --> 00:00:39,012
But what happened to artisan, hand-crafted firmware development?

8
00:00:39,012 --> 00:00:47,149
If you aren't physically mashing sixteen silicone pads ten thousand times until your thumbs go numb, can you even look yourself in the mirror?

9
00:00:47,804 --> 00:00:51,316
And think of your remote open source contributors.

10
00:00:51,266 --> 00:01:01,266
Giving them a zero-install web URL where the entire seven-inch touchscreen, velocity pads, and ComProtocol bridge run instantly at sixty frames a second?

11
00:01:01,266 --> 00:01:03,904
Where is the character-building struggle?

12
00:01:03,904 --> 00:01:14,329
It is much more romantic to ship fifty fragile developer boards through international customs, have three lost in transit, and wait six weeks for replacement tactile switches.

13
00:01:14,992 --> 00:01:20,517
So keep your dangling jumper wires, your multimeter, and your fire extinguisher.

14
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:38,179
But for the rest of us who prefer shipping working firmware over ordering replacement motherboards, the 0700-AAM Sovereign Web Simulator is live right now in four K on GitHub Pages at rasrobo.github.io/0700-aam-sim.

15
00:01:38,179 --> 00:01:40,617
Zero solder required.
